WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.294
Configuration values should not appear in user-facing errors, logs, telemetry, traces, health checks, crash reports, screenshots, or support bundles when those values are sensitive.

2
00:00:12.294 --> 00:00:24.113
This includes connection strings, tokens, headers, keys, certificate material, provider-specific secrets, internal paths, and sometimes database names or account identifiers.

3
00:00:24.113 --> 00:00:28.799
Diagnostics should separate user visibility from operator visibility.

4
00:00:28.799 --> 00:00:32.467
Users need safe error messages and support references.

5
00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:43.674
Authorized operators may need more detail, but that detail should be protected, redacted, retained appropriately, and limited to people with a real operational need.

6
00:00:43.674 --> 00:00:46.527
Structured logging and redaction can help.

7
00:00:46.527 --> 00:00:57.463
Logs can capture setting categories, configuration source names, validation outcomes, correlation IDs, and safe state transitions without printing secret values.

8
00:00:57.463 --> 00:01:04.663
Avoid logging entire configuration objects because safe and sensitive values often sit next to each other.

9
00:01:04.663 --> 00:01:09.553
Health checks and status pages are useful, but they can expose too much.

10
00:01:09.553 --> 00:01:17.636
A health endpoint may reveal provider names, dependency status, internal routes, storage names, or environment details.

11
00:01:17.636 --> 00:01:25.175
Decide what is safe for public, internal, and operator-only audiences, and configure each endpoint accordingly.

12
00:01:25.175 --> 00:01:28.775
Development and production diagnostics should differ.

13
00:01:28.775 --> 00:01:31.628
Detailed local errors can help developers.

14
00:01:31.628 --> 00:01:40.118
Production diagnostics should prioritize safe user messages, protected logs, monitoring signals, and correlation identifiers.

15
00:01:40.118 --> 00:01:45.756
Copying development diagnostic behavior into production creates avoidable exposure.

16
00:01:45.756 --> 00:01:49.967
Correlation IDs support investigation without leaking secrets.

17
00:01:49.967 --> 00:02:00.156
They let support teams and operators connect user reports to protected logs while keeping sensitive configuration values out of user-visible channels.

18
00:02:00.156 --> 00:02:04.367
Good diagnostics are useful because they are bounded and safe.
